window.docContents[193].resolve({"/part-2-analysis/ch07-continuity/#ch07-continuity":{"contents":"A function f : A \\to \\mathbb{R} is continuous at c \\in A if and only if its nonstandard extension {}^*f maps the halo of c into the halo of f(c):\n\n\\forall x \\in {}^*A,\\quad x \\approx_h c \\implies {}^*f(x) \\approx_h f(c).\n\nNotice the difference between pointwise continuity and uniform continuity:\n\n1. In ordinary continuity, c \\in A is fixed to be a standard real first, and x \\in {}^*A varies near c.2. In uniform continuity on A, both x, y \\in {}^*A range freely over all hyperreals in {}^*A: x \\approx_h y \\implies {}^*f(x) \\approx_h {}^*f(y).\n\nWhy does f(x) = x^2 fail uniform continuity on \\mathbb{R}? Take an unlimited \\omega and \\varepsilon = 1/\\omega \\approx 0: then \\omega + \\varepsilon \\approx_h \\omega, yet (\\omega + \\varepsilon)^2 - \\omega^2 = 2 + \\varepsilon^2 \\not\\approx_h 0!\n\nAnd why is every continuous function on a compact interval [a, b] automatically uniformly continuous (Heine–Cantor)? Let x, y \\in {}^*[a, b] satisfy x \\approx_h y. Since a \\le x \\le b, x is limited, so c = \\mathrm{sh}(x) lies in [a, b]! Then x \\approx_h c \\approx_h y, and continuity at the single real point c yields\n\n{}^*f(x) \\approx_h f(c) \\approx_h {}^*f(y).\n\nThe entire Heine–Cantor proof is six lines long! In Ch07_ContinuousFunctions.lean (2,847 lines), we also formalize Thomae's popcorn function, the Intermediate and Extreme Value Theorems via hyperfinite grids, and Augustin-Louis Cauchy's famous 1821 controversy over infinite series of continuous functions—pinpointing the missing hypothesis of micro-uniform convergence at unlimited indices.\n\nsection\nopen Goldblatt.Ch07\n\n-- Thomae's function, nonstandard IVT/EVT, and Cauchy's 1821 series theorem:\n#check @continuousAt_thomaeFunction_iff\n#check @intermediate_value_Icc_nonstandard\n#check @extreme_value_Icc_nonstandard\n#check @cauchySeries_counterexample\n#check @cauchy_sum_theorem_nonstandard\nend\n\n\n","context":"Lectures on the Hyperreals — Interactive Lean 4 & Spatial Edition\u0009Part II — Elementary Analysis via Infinitesimals","header":"2.2. Chapter 7: Microcontinuity, Heine–Cantor & Cauchy's 1821 Sum Theorem","id":"/part-2-analysis/ch07-continuity/#ch07-continuity"}});